course: everyday
lesson: 333
title: Saying no honestly, harmful lies, cheating in games and tests, jokes and teasing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sometimes a child does not want to go to a party and says «I'm sick» when they are not. There is no need to invent a reason: «Thanks, but I can't come» is honest and polite, because you do not owe everyone a reason.
explain: Some lies cause real harm. In «The Boy Who Cried Wolf», a shepherd boy shouts «Wolf!» as a joke again and again; when a real wolf comes, nobody believes him and nobody comes. Lying to a doctor about how you got hurt, or prank calling an emergency number, can stop real help from reaching the person who needs it.
explain: Games and tests only work when players are honest: the banker in Monopoly handles the money fairly, a tagged player admits it and becomes «it», a footballer does not fake a fall, and a pupil tells the teacher if a wrong sum gave them extra marks.
explain: Kind jokes, like knock-knock jokes with a younger brother or sister, build friendship. Teasing someone about their accent or the language they speak at home is unkind, and you can stand up for them or stay with them.
fact: You do not owe everyone a reason for saying no, so you rarely need to invent one; «Thanks, but I can't come» is enough.
fact: Pretending to be hurt to get attention is a false alarm, and false alarms make it harder to be believed later.
fact: In «The Boy Who Cried Wolf», when the boy really needed help nobody came, because he had lied before.
fact: Tell doctors and nurses the truth about how you got hurt; they need true facts to treat you, and they are there to help, not to punish.
fact: Never prank call 911, 999, 112, 000 or 111; it ties up help that someone in a real emergency needs, and it is against the law.
fact: Being the banker in a game is a test of honesty, even though it is only a game.
fact: Games rely on players being honest about what happened, so a tagged player admits it and becomes «it».
fact: Faking a fall in football to win a free kick is called diving; it is cheating and can earn a yellow card.
fact: Telling a teacher about a mistake that gave you a better mark shows real integrity.
fact: Sharing silly jokes, like knock-knock jokes, builds friendship and helps younger children learn language.
fact: Speaking more than one language is a skill, and mocking someone's accent is unkind.
fact: If a «joke» hurts the person it is about, it is not a joke but unkind teasing; if it goes on, tell an adult.
exercise: A boy does not want to go to a classmate's party, so he says he is sick when he is fine. What could he say instead? | «Thanks, but I can't come» | «I have a broken leg, sorry» | «My dog ate the invitation» | Nothing, and just not turn up
exercise: Why do you rarely need to invent a reason to say no to a party? | You do not owe everyone a reason | Hosts always check your excuse with your parents | Polite people never say no | Made-up excuses are more polite
exercise: A girl often says she is hurt to get attention when she is not. What is the danger? | People may not believe her when she really is hurt | There is no danger if she smiles afterwards and says sorry | She will be given more presents | Her friends will always believe her
exercise: In «The Boy Who Cried Wolf», why did nobody come when the real wolf came? | He had given false alarms before, so nobody believed him | Nobody could hear him over all the noise of the sheep in the field | The villagers were all asleep | The wolf was too quiet to notice
exercise: A boy broke his finger climbing a fence he was told to keep off. He is scared and wants to tell the doctor he fell over. What should he do? | Tell the doctor the truth about what happened | Say he fell over, since it is close enough to the truth | Refuse to say anything at all | Say his brother pushed him
exercise: Why should you tell a doctor the truth about how you got hurt? | Doctors need true facts to treat you properly | Doctors can tell anyway, so it does not matter | Doctors write it on your school report | It makes the medicine taste better
exercise: A child in California calls 911 as a joke. Why is this wrong? | It ties up help for someone in a real emergency, and it is against the law | It is fine if you hang up quickly, because then the call does not count at all | It only costs a few cents of phone credit | The operator will just laugh along
exercise: You are the banker in Monopoly and could slip yourself extra money. What is right? | Handle the bank honestly | Take a little, since it is only a game | Take extra but give some to a friend | Take some only if you are losing
exercise: Why does honesty matter even when you are the banker in a game? | Being trusted with the money is a test of honesty | Games have a judge who checks the bank after every round | The banker always wins anyway | Extra money is against the box rules only
exercise: In tag, a girl is clearly touched but says she wasn't. What should she do? | Admit it and become «it» | Say the touch was too soft | Run home so the game stops | Say she was on base
exercise: A player falls on purpose in football to win a free kick. Which is TRUE? | It is cheating called diving and can earn a yellow card | It is a clever skill that coaches teach to all good players | It is allowed if the referee does not see | It is only wrong in a final
exercise: The teacher added your marks wrongly and gave you more than you earned. What should you do? | Tell the teacher about the mistake | Keep quiet, since it was not your mistake | Tell only your best friend | Correct it only if it gave you less
exercise: Your little sister tells you a very silly knock-knock joke. What is the kind thing to do? | Play along and laugh with her | Tell her it is the worst joke ever | Ignore her until she stops talking | Tell her jokes are only for babies
exercise: Why are silly jokes with younger children good? | They build friendship and help young children learn language | They make the younger child feel small and silly in front of others | They teach children to stop talking | Only funny jokes are worth sharing
exercise: A classmate is laughed at for her accent and the language she speaks at home. What should you do? | Stand up for her or stay with her | Join in so they do not laugh at you | Walk away and pretend you saw nothing | Tell her to stop speaking her language
exercise: Which is TRUE about speaking more than one language? | It is a skill to be proud of | It means you cannot speak English well | It should be hidden at school | It is something to make fun of
test: Your aunt asks if you want to come to a long concert, and you do not. Which reply is both honest and polite? | «Thank you, but I don't think I'll come» | «I have a terrible illness that day, sorry» | «My teacher said I must stay home» | «I would love to», and then not going
test: A boy shouts «Help, I'm drowning!» in a pool three times as a joke. Why is that dangerous for him later? | The lifeguard may not hurry when he really is in trouble | It is only a problem if he shouts it in the sea, not in a pool | Lifeguards always come every time anyway | It makes him a better swimmer
test: A girl in Ireland calls 112 for a dare. What is the most important harm? | Someone in real danger may wait longer for help | Her phone may stop working | The operator's lunch is delayed | Nobody is harmed if it is short
test: A girl got burned using the stove without asking. At the hospital she is scared of getting in trouble. Why is the truth best? | The nurse needs to know the real cause to treat the burn properly | Burns heal exactly the same whatever she tells the nurse about them | The nurse will punish her if she lies | The nurse already saw what happened
test: In hide-and-seek, a boy is spotted but quietly moves and says «You never saw me». What is wrong? | Games only work when players are honest about what happened | Nothing is wrong, because moving quietly is part of the game | He was too slow to be found anyway | It is fine if he wins
test: A hockey player in Canada falls over loudly when nobody touched her. Which is TRUE? | Faking a foul is cheating, even if the referee is fooled | It is fine if her team is losing | It is a normal part of the game | Only the referee is cheating here
test: A girl notices she got 10 out of 10 on a test where she got one sum wrong. Why is telling the teacher a sign of real integrity? | She is honest even when the mistake helps her | She wants to make the teacher look bad in front of the class | She will get a prize for it | She is afraid of being found out
test: Some children laugh when a new boy says words with a Scottish accent. Which is TRUE? | Mocking an accent is unkind, and you can stand up for him | Accents are funny, so laughing is fine as long as he laughs too | He should change the way he talks | It is only teasing if he cries
test: A five-year-old tells you «Knock knock!» for the tenth time today. Why is playing along still a good thing? | It builds your friendship and helps him learn language | It will stop him ever telling jokes again, so it is worth it | Only clever jokes help children learn | It makes the joke funnier for adults
explain: A boy in New York prank-called 911 for fun; the line was busy for a real emergency nearby, and police later came to his house.
explain: A girl in England kept faking injuries in football to win free kicks; when she was really hurt, the referee did not believe her at first.
fact: Prank calls to emergency numbers can delay help for someone who is really in danger.
fact: When someone lies often, others stop believing them even when they tell the truth.
test: dial 999 for a laugh then hang up | Never; it can delay help for someone | It's fine if you hang up straight away | It's fine from a payphone | It's fine once
test: faked a fall to get a penalty and it worked lol | It's cheating, and people stop believing you | Do it every match | Only fake it in big games | Teach your mates
test: kids calling him names and saying it's just a joke | If it hurts, it's not a joke; tell an adult | Join in so they leave you alone next time | It's fine if they laugh | Ignore it forever
test: did u hear a kid kept faking stomach aches and when "his appendix actually went, his mum thought he was faking". lesson? | False alarms make it harder to be believed later | Faking is fine if you're bored | Mums always know the truth | Stomach aches are never real
test: my mate says a boy prank called 999 and "his family got fined a million pounds". what's prob exaggerated | The million; but prank calls are against the law and delay help | That 999 is only for real emergencies | That prank calls are wrong | That it's against the law
test: kids say the new boy "talks funny, he's from nowhere". pass it on? | No, speaking more than one language is a skill; don't mock it | Yes, he talks funny | Yes, and copy his accent | Yes, it's just a joke
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the lesson of «The Boy Who Cried Wolf»? | One who lies often is not believed when truly in need | Liars are always believed in the end, however often they lie | Wolves attack only shepherds | Crying for help is always wrong
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person need not invent an excuse when declining an invitation. | One does not owe everyone a reason for saying no | Excuses are always discovered by the host in the end | Excuses are prohibited by law | Invitations cannot be refused
exam: It is given that a teacher has made an error in a pupil's favour. Select the response consistent with integrity. | The pupil tells the teacher | The pupil keeps the error secret | The pupil boasts of the mark | The pupil asks for more marks
exam: State the effect of sharing silly jokes with younger children. | It builds friendship and helps them learn language | It confuses them and slows their learning of words | It has no effect | It teaches them to be rude
